One feature of PyMC 2 that might be hard to replicate in v.3 that has been enormously useful is the creating of arbitrary stochastics (and determinsitics, for that matter) using the @stochastic (or @observed for likelihoods) decorator. It comes in handy for adding non-standard distributions to a model. For example, in survival modeling, the log-likelihood for the parametric survival model with exponential hazard is:
@observed
def survival(value=t, lam=lam, f=failure):
    """Exponential survival likelihood, accounting for censoring"""
    return sum(f*log(lam) - lam*value)

This allows the user the ability to easily specify a stochastic node without building a class by hand.
